1
00:00:00,767 --> 00:00:03,293
<i>- Previously on</i> Gotham...
- Shut up and die.

2
00:00:04,705 --> 00:00:06,230
OSWALD: <i>Say hello to Butch.</i>

3
00:00:06,373 --> 00:00:07,102
(grunts)

4
00:00:07,241 --> 00:00:08,470
No!

5
00:00:08,642 --> 00:00:09,405
(gunshot)

6
00:00:09,543 --> 00:00:12,240
BRUCE: <i>She's paralyzed
and has lost the will to live.</i>

7
00:00:12,379 --> 00:00:14,057
IVY: <i>If Selina ingests
this, it'll find its way</i>

8
00:00:14,081 --> 00:00:16,209
<i>- to her wound.</i>
- SELINA: <i>I'm better.</i>

9
00:00:16,350 --> 00:00:18,376
<i>So now I have to find
the freak who shot me.</i>

10
00:00:18,519 --> 00:00:22,081
ECCO: <i>Welcome to
the Church of Jeremiah.</i>

11
00:00:22,756 --> 00:00:24,054
Selina!

12
00:00:24,191 --> 00:00:25,435
NYGMA: <i>This time,
woke up on a rooftop.</i>

13
00:00:25,459 --> 00:00:26,483
<i>No idea how I got there</i>

14
00:00:26,627 --> 00:00:28,137
<i>or what happened during
the preceding hours.</i>

15
00:00:28,161 --> 00:00:29,161
Who the hell are you?

16
00:00:29,263 --> 00:00:30,263
You don't remember?

17
00:00:30,497 --> 00:00:31,624
What is happening to me?

18
00:00:31,798 --> 00:00:34,063
Gonna need people I can
trust watching over this place.

19
00:00:34,201 --> 00:00:36,670
- You could be my deputy
here in Haven.
- Thanks.

20
00:00:36,803 --> 00:00:39,272
OSWALD: <i>Well, if
it isn't my old friend</i>

21
00:00:39,406 --> 00:00:40,430
Mr. Penn.

22
00:00:40,574 --> 00:00:42,167
You shouldn't have
come here, Oswald.

23
00:00:42,309 --> 00:00:44,020
Perhaps you shouldn't
have stolen my people.

24
00:00:44,044 --> 00:00:44,704
BARBARA: Hi, Pengy.

25
00:00:45,045 --> 00:00:46,240
- OSWALD:
Jim!
- Barbara, no.

26
00:00:52,052 --> 00:00:53,748
♪♪

27
00:00:59,259 --> 00:01:01,455
(child crying)

28
00:01:04,731 --> 00:01:07,098
(groaning)

29
00:01:10,504 --> 00:01:12,405
(coughing)

30
00:01:13,073 --> 00:01:15,736
(gasping)

31
00:01:21,815 --> 00:01:23,716
(screaming)

32
00:01:26,320 --> 00:01:28,516
(coughing)

33
00:01:31,091 --> 00:01:32,091
(coughs)

34
00:01:36,263 --> 00:01:39,233
(shuddering)

35
00:01:39,499 --> 00:01:41,263
(muffled chatter)

36
00:01:41,568 --> 00:01:44,265
(baby crying)

37
00:01:44,438 --> 00:01:45,531
(woman crying)

38
00:01:48,475 --> 00:01:49,475
GORDON: Over here!

39
00:01:49,509 --> 00:01:51,478
- (coughs)
- Over here!

40
00:01:51,612 --> 00:01:52,636
Over here!

41
00:01:52,779 --> 00:01:54,338
This woman needs
medical attention.

42
00:01:54,481 --> 00:01:56,609
Find a doctor. Go. Hey, hey!

43
00:01:57,351 --> 00:01:58,580
You! Hey, listen up.

44
00:01:59,319 --> 00:02:01,788
Pair up. I need teams
going in looking for survivors.

45
00:02:02,122 --> 00:02:04,148
- Go. Get those people back!
- (siren wailing)

46
00:02:04,291 --> 00:02:05,816
Hey. You.

47
00:02:06,159 --> 00:02:07,718
Set up a triage over here.

48
00:02:11,565 --> 00:02:13,397
Hey. Hey, come on. Snap to.

49
00:02:13,700 --> 00:02:15,692
Focus. There's gonna
be a lot of wounded.

50
00:02:16,236 --> 00:02:17,602
Look at me. Look at me.

51
00:02:17,871 --> 00:02:20,340
All right. Find all the
medical supplies you can. Go.

52
00:02:24,277 --> 00:02:26,143
You did this. You
brought the gangs here!

53
00:02:26,279 --> 00:02:27,907
No! I would never allow that.

54
00:02:28,248 --> 00:02:30,308
None of them had
explosives, that I know of.

55
00:02:30,450 --> 00:02:31,247
BULLOCK: Jim!

56
00:02:31,385 --> 00:02:32,729
We need to get
these fires out, Harvey.

57
00:02:32,753 --> 00:02:34,381
We got nothing to put hoses on.

58
00:02:35,322 --> 00:02:36,522
Use the drinking water. Do it.

59
00:02:37,491 --> 00:02:38,652
Hey. Hey!

60
00:02:38,792 --> 00:02:41,227
Get those hoses over here now!

61
00:02:52,706 --> 00:02:54,937
(indistinct chatter,
siren wailing in distance)

62
00:03:02,883 --> 00:03:03,883
Jim.

63
00:03:04,918 --> 00:03:06,853
Harvey. Flames are dying.

64
00:03:07,187 --> 00:03:08,519
We need more teams inside.

65
00:03:09,222 --> 00:03:10,742
- There have to be survivors.
- I know.

66
00:03:11,358 --> 00:03:14,726
It's just, an officer
just found this.

67
00:03:17,297 --> 00:03:18,424
It's yours.

68
00:03:30,777 --> 00:03:32,678
♪♪

69
00:03:41,488 --> 00:03:45,516
GORDON: <i>As of now,
the death toll stands at 311.</i>

70
00:03:47,294 --> 00:03:48,557
49 injured.

71
00:03:49,463 --> 00:03:51,295
More than two dozen
unaccounted for.

72
00:03:51,765 --> 00:03:53,927
WALKER: I'm so
terribly sorry, Captain.

73
00:03:54,601 --> 00:03:56,661
Do you have any idea
who's responsible?

74
00:03:57,370 --> 00:03:59,532
There was an incursion
by several of the gangs.

75
00:03:59,673 --> 00:04:01,505
Any one of them
could've done it.

76
00:04:01,775 --> 00:04:03,869
Right now, it's impossible
to rule anyone out.

77
00:04:04,578 --> 00:04:06,444
I'm working on getting you help.

78
00:04:06,580 --> 00:04:08,446
You've been promising
me help for weeks.

79
00:04:08,849 --> 00:04:11,011
I have bodies lined
up in the streets.

80
00:04:11,752 --> 00:04:14,017
You have every right
to be upset, Captain.

81
00:04:14,354 --> 00:04:15,932
- But I'm doing
everything I can...
- Yeah.

82
00:04:15,956 --> 00:04:17,356
To make sure
that you and your...

83
00:04:17,424 --> 00:04:19,916
(shouting in distance)

84
00:04:31,004 --> 00:04:32,336
Let them through.

85
00:04:32,939 --> 00:04:34,635
- There he is!
- Let them through!

86
00:04:34,808 --> 00:04:37,039
Let them through.
It's okay. It's all right.

87
00:04:37,410 --> 00:04:39,436
MAN: When are you
gonna find out who did this?

88
00:04:39,579 --> 00:04:40,808
My family was in there.

89
00:04:40,947 --> 00:04:42,609
How do we know we're safe here?

90
00:04:42,749 --> 00:04:43,773
GORDON: I know...

91
00:04:45,585 --> 00:04:49,545
I know each one of you
lost someone you care for.

92
00:04:50,791 --> 00:04:52,350
I share your grief.

93
00:04:52,726 --> 00:04:54,285
I share your anger.

94
00:04:55,328 --> 00:04:56,688
But whoever
destroyed that building

95
00:04:56,730 --> 00:04:58,756
can't destroy the
hope we've built.

96
00:04:59,766 --> 00:05:01,564
Not unless we let them.

97
00:05:02,369 --> 00:05:03,428
I swear,

98
00:05:04,337 --> 00:05:07,899
I promise I won't stop until
I find those responsible.

99
00:05:09,776 --> 00:05:10,971
Justice will be done.

100
00:05:11,444 --> 00:05:13,572
How are you gonna stop
it from happening again?

101
00:05:13,713 --> 00:05:15,443
(crowd shouting)

102
00:05:23,957 --> 00:05:24,957
It was a nice try.

103
00:05:25,659 --> 00:05:26,979
Can you blame
them? They're angry.

104
00:05:27,360 --> 00:05:28,640
It's gonna take more than words.

105
00:05:28,762 --> 00:05:30,424
Someone has to
pay for this, soon.

106
00:05:30,564 --> 00:05:32,897
Well, the department's not
exactly firing on all cylinders.

107
00:05:33,033 --> 00:05:35,433
We got more men than bullets,
and we ain't got that many men.

108
00:05:35,535 --> 00:05:38,027
Lucius, I need something.
Comb through every piece

109
00:05:38,371 --> 00:05:39,371
of evidence you can find.

110
00:05:39,506 --> 00:05:40,817
I need to know who
did this, and how.

111
00:05:40,841 --> 00:05:42,852
It's not gonna be easy. The
size of the crime scene alone...

112
00:05:42,876 --> 00:05:44,796
Do whatever you have
to do. The clock is ticking.

113
00:05:45,645 --> 00:05:46,806
More than you know.

114
00:05:47,414 --> 00:05:50,009
When Barbara and I showed
up, that's when the building blew.

115
00:05:50,617 --> 00:05:51,949
She vanished right after.

116
00:05:54,020 --> 00:05:55,818
This doesn't make
sense. Not even for her.

117
00:05:55,956 --> 00:05:59,518
Nothing makes sense anymore.
You're in charge, Harvey.

118
00:06:02,128 --> 00:06:03,858
BRUCE: Selina!

119
00:06:17,911 --> 00:06:18,911
Oh.

120
00:06:20,513 --> 00:06:21,513
Hey.

121
00:06:44,104 --> 00:06:45,663
I was afraid you
didn't get my signal.

122
00:06:46,573 --> 00:06:48,508
Lucius said the range
was only a couple miles.

123
00:06:48,775 --> 00:06:51,643
Oh, you know me, Master Bruce,
I managed to muddle through.

124
00:06:54,147 --> 00:06:56,844
So go on, then.
How did that happen?

125
00:06:58,718 --> 00:06:59,913
I let my guard down.

126
00:07:00,153 --> 00:07:01,731
You're telling me that
your captors shackle you,

127
00:07:01,755 --> 00:07:02,832
they leave a radio transponder

128
00:07:02,856 --> 00:07:05,690
in your pocket and...
and your weapons?

129
00:07:05,825 --> 00:07:06,825
Really?

130
00:07:07,761 --> 00:07:09,423
I think you're telling porkies.

131
00:07:10,730 --> 00:07:11,823
It was Selina.

132
00:07:12,465 --> 00:07:14,764
She's gone after
Jeremiah, alone.

133
00:07:15,802 --> 00:07:17,430
You want to follow her, do you?

134
00:07:18,505 --> 00:07:20,804
She's not herself, Alfred.
She's desperate for revenge.

135
00:07:21,041 --> 00:07:22,976
She's gonna get
herself hurt, or killed.

136
00:07:23,109 --> 00:07:24,941
Seems to be a little
bit of a recurring theme

137
00:07:25,111 --> 00:07:26,545
with madam, isn't it?

138
00:07:27,213 --> 00:07:28,991
You know, one of these
days you're gonna realize

139
00:07:29,015 --> 00:07:31,814
you can't save her from herself.

140
00:07:33,853 --> 00:07:35,116
Until that day comes...

141
00:07:39,059 --> 00:07:40,994
I think we should go
and find her, don't you?

142
00:07:53,740 --> 00:07:54,867
(gun clicks)

143
00:07:57,143 --> 00:07:58,543
BARBARA: Let him in.

144
00:08:00,814 --> 00:08:02,840
You're either here
for information,

145
00:08:04,084 --> 00:08:06,110
or you somehow think I
might've had something to do

146
00:08:06,252 --> 00:08:07,550
with that atrocity.

147
00:08:07,687 --> 00:08:09,178
Penguin murdered Tabitha.

148
00:08:09,522 --> 00:08:10,785
You knew he was at Haven.

149
00:08:11,558 --> 00:08:12,835
You would've done
anything for revenge.

150
00:08:12,859 --> 00:08:14,459
And I could've killed
him then and there.

151
00:08:14,527 --> 00:08:15,586
So why didn't you?

152
00:08:17,731 --> 00:08:20,565
Because the person who blew
up Haven has to be stopped.

153
00:08:21,568 --> 00:08:22,968
That's all that matters now.

154
00:08:23,870 --> 00:08:25,805
You're worried
Sirens could be next.

155
00:08:27,007 --> 00:08:28,703
I've been putting out feelers.

156
00:08:30,076 --> 00:08:31,544
Is that what that was?

157
00:08:31,945 --> 00:08:33,709
He heard people
talking about a shady guy

158
00:08:33,847 --> 00:08:35,577
lurking around
Haven before it blew.

159
00:08:35,949 --> 00:08:36,780
This is Gotham.

160
00:08:36,916 --> 00:08:39,010
You're gonna have to do
better than "shady guy."

161
00:08:39,252 --> 00:08:40,686
How about a location?

162
00:08:41,287 --> 00:08:43,688
A building in the northeast
corner of Harlow Park.

163
00:08:43,923 --> 00:08:45,653
He says the guy's
holed up there.

164
00:08:47,560 --> 00:08:48,994
How much is this gonna cost me?

165
00:08:50,063 --> 00:08:51,156
Just do your job.

166
00:08:51,698 --> 00:08:53,030
Get the bastard.

167
00:08:59,806 --> 00:09:02,241
Did you really think I could've
murdered all those people?

168
00:09:04,711 --> 00:09:06,009
I don't know.

169
00:09:08,548 --> 00:09:10,039
I guess I deserve that.

170
00:09:11,584 --> 00:09:12,624
BULLOCK (over radio): Jim.

171
00:09:13,653 --> 00:09:14,653
Jim.

172
00:09:15,989 --> 00:09:16,989
Jim, you read me?

173
00:09:17,057 --> 00:09:18,057
Yeah.

174
00:09:18,725 --> 00:09:19,886
We got a problem.

175
00:09:21,728 --> 00:09:22,889
On my way.

176
00:09:32,205 --> 00:09:35,903
(door opens, closes)

177
00:09:38,812 --> 00:09:39,871
Oswald?

178
00:09:42,082 --> 00:09:43,345
What are you doing here?

179
00:09:43,917 --> 00:09:48,321
Jim, it has become
woefully apparent

180
00:09:48,655 --> 00:09:51,124
that you are outmanned,

181
00:09:51,257 --> 00:09:53,817
outgunned and out of options.

182
00:09:55,929 --> 00:09:57,124
So I'm here to help.

183
00:10:00,700 --> 00:10:01,700
That's generous of you.

184
00:10:01,801 --> 00:10:03,633
(laughs): I'm just
getting started.

185
00:10:09,943 --> 00:10:11,002
BULLOCK: Well.

186
00:10:11,244 --> 00:10:13,907
Ain't you just Saint Nick
on a Christmas morning?

187
00:10:14,214 --> 00:10:16,683
Don't be shy. Go.
Take all you can carry.

188
00:10:24,757 --> 00:10:26,783
I'm guessing there
are strings attached?

189
00:10:27,760 --> 00:10:28,784
No strings.

190
00:10:28,995 --> 00:10:30,395
Save for the one
that we will cinch

191
00:10:30,730 --> 00:10:32,255
around the neck of
the Haven bomber.

192
00:10:32,999 --> 00:10:35,161
I lost people, too, Jim.

193
00:10:35,702 --> 00:10:39,298
People you lured with
promises of safety and security,

194
00:10:39,806 --> 00:10:41,206
only to have them incinerated.

195
00:10:41,341 --> 00:10:45,278
But let's just hold that in
abeyance for the moment.

196
00:10:45,945 --> 00:10:49,143
I would rather we put aside
our considerable differences,

197
00:10:50,083 --> 00:10:51,847
and go get the
people some justice.

198
00:10:54,287 --> 00:10:55,846
What do you say, partner?

199
00:11:08,034 --> 00:11:10,401
(sighs)

200
00:11:37,830 --> 00:11:38,923
Apparently,

201
00:11:39,866 --> 00:11:41,858
I've been on a trip,
or I'm going on one.

202
00:11:44,103 --> 00:11:46,004
I've also left myself a message.

203
00:11:46,739 --> 00:11:49,766
"In A-T number 1215 knows."

204
00:11:49,976 --> 00:11:51,911
In... nate?

205
00:11:52,178 --> 00:11:55,046
In... grate? Inflate?

206
00:11:55,782 --> 00:11:56,943
In... mate?

207
00:11:57,350 --> 00:11:59,114
"Inmate number 1215 knows."

208
00:11:59,319 --> 00:12:00,912
Inmate number 1215 knows!

209
00:12:02,789 --> 00:12:04,348
Knows what?! (groans)

210
00:12:04,490 --> 00:12:06,482
Okay. Okay, think.

211
00:12:07,126 --> 00:12:09,061
Prison records. Where
do I get prison records?

212
00:12:09,762 --> 00:12:10,821
(sighs)

213
00:12:12,932 --> 00:12:13,932
Right.

214
00:12:19,939 --> 00:12:21,840
♪♪

215
00:12:30,450 --> 00:12:31,247
To hell with Penguin.

216
00:12:31,384 --> 00:12:32,408
Haven wasn't your fault.

217
00:12:33,186 --> 00:12:34,745
I told the people it was safe.

218
00:12:34,887 --> 00:12:35,927
I made them into a target.

219
00:12:36,089 --> 00:12:36,954
Jim, you gave 'em...

220
00:12:37,090 --> 00:12:39,992
Don't tell me I gave
'em hope. They're dead.

221
00:12:41,261 --> 00:12:43,924
All right, fan out.

222
00:12:45,231 --> 00:12:47,860
We go building to
building, door to door.

223
00:12:48,334 --> 00:12:51,168
Our source said this is
where we'll find the bomber.

224
00:12:51,871 --> 00:12:53,237
He's here somewhere.

225
00:12:53,840 --> 00:12:56,173
Alvarez, take a squad, grab
the north side of the street.

226
00:12:56,309 --> 00:12:58,369
The rest of you,
cover the south.

227
00:12:59,145 --> 00:13:00,977
To the vile miscreant

228
00:13:01,114 --> 00:13:03,106
who murdered those
innocent Havenites,

229
00:13:03,483 --> 00:13:05,247
you have nowhere to run!

230
00:13:06,019 --> 00:13:07,419
There goes the
element of surprise.

231
00:13:07,854 --> 00:13:09,254
OSWALD: We will root you out

232
00:13:09,389 --> 00:13:11,187
like the vermin you are!

233
00:13:11,791 --> 00:13:13,987
Two forces united
in one purpose!

234
00:13:14,127 --> 00:13:15,127
(gunshot)

235
00:13:15,161 --> 00:13:17,130
- Get back! Get back!
- Get down!

236
00:13:24,237 --> 00:13:26,263
GORDON: Elevated
position, back to the sun.

237
00:13:26,806 --> 00:13:28,035
(gun cocks)

238
00:13:29,809 --> 00:13:31,449
BULLOCK: Looks like
we found our guy, huh?

239
00:13:32,412 --> 00:13:33,412
(gunshot)

240
00:13:35,848 --> 00:13:36,925
We're sitting ducks out here.

241
00:13:36,949 --> 00:13:37,949
BULLOCK: And one Penguin.

242
00:13:38,051 --> 00:13:39,428
Hey, Oswald, why
don't you crawl out there,

243
00:13:39,452 --> 00:13:41,546
grab that bullhorn and tell
him to come down quietly?

244
00:13:43,323 --> 00:13:44,323
(gunshot)

245
00:13:45,491 --> 00:13:48,290
GORDON: Put your weapons down!
We have the building surrounded.

246
00:13:48,594 --> 00:13:50,460
And we have more ammo than you!

247
00:13:50,596 --> 00:13:52,827
Come out now with
your hands in the air.

248
00:13:54,200 --> 00:13:55,395
MAN: Pretty cozy up here.

249
00:13:56,069 --> 00:13:57,128
Thanks, guys.

250
00:14:00,840 --> 00:14:02,035
I know that voice.

251
00:14:03,042 --> 00:14:04,101
Zsasz?

252
00:14:05,044 --> 00:14:06,410
Oh, hey, guys. What's up?

253
00:14:07,013 --> 00:14:08,013
(gunshot)

254
00:14:32,972 --> 00:14:34,873
(door creaks)

255
00:14:40,546 --> 00:14:41,546
Oh.

256
00:14:53,526 --> 00:14:55,017
It's impolite to
sneak up on people.

257
00:14:55,161 --> 00:14:56,390
So is breaking and entering.

258
00:14:58,030 --> 00:14:59,259
Heard you were dead, Ed.

259
00:14:59,399 --> 00:15:00,628
Now what are you looking for?

260
00:15:01,234 --> 00:15:03,897
I am given, and I am taken.

261
00:15:04,303 --> 00:15:05,999
I was there from
your first breath,

262
00:15:06,205 --> 00:15:09,175
and I will follow
you until your death.

263
00:15:10,009 --> 00:15:11,409
(exhales) A name.

264
00:15:13,012 --> 00:15:14,036
So...

265
00:15:15,047 --> 00:15:18,017
you're looking for the name
of a prisoner at Blackgate.

266
00:15:19,685 --> 00:15:21,210
Call it a personal matter.

267
00:15:21,921 --> 00:15:23,981
- This folder's quite valuable.
- Right.

268
00:15:24,490 --> 00:15:26,550
What is the going
rate on dust bunnies?

269
00:15:27,093 --> 00:15:28,893
What I mean to say is,
this folder seems to be

270
00:15:28,995 --> 00:15:30,554
quite valuable to you.

271
00:15:33,533 --> 00:15:34,533
Well...

272
00:15:35,401 --> 00:15:36,478
I'm guessing you
don't want money,

273
00:15:36,502 --> 00:15:38,368
because, uh, it's worthless.

274
00:15:38,938 --> 00:15:40,600
I don't tend to
carry snacks on me.

275
00:15:41,340 --> 00:15:42,706
And if I had any bullets,

276
00:15:43,042 --> 00:15:44,253
I would just shoot
you and take the folder.

277
00:15:44,277 --> 00:15:46,007
So what is it that
you would like?

278
00:15:46,345 --> 00:15:48,041
Your expertise.

279
00:15:49,215 --> 00:15:49,978
Surely you've noticed

280
00:15:50,116 --> 00:15:51,948
the giant plume of
smoke in the sky?

281
00:15:52,685 --> 00:15:56,019
Someone blew up a building
we were using to house refugees.

282
00:15:56,422 --> 00:15:57,902
We need to figure
out how they did it,

283
00:15:58,024 --> 00:16:00,220
so we can prevent
them from doing it again.

284
00:16:01,494 --> 00:16:04,487
So, the second smartest
man in Gotham...

285
00:16:05,965 --> 00:16:07,126
needs my help.

286
00:16:07,333 --> 00:16:09,097
Explosives are not my expertise.

287
00:16:09,235 --> 00:16:10,430
Didn't realize you had one.

288
00:16:10,536 --> 00:16:11,536
Hmm.

289
00:16:14,640 --> 00:16:15,640
Just...

290
00:16:16,309 --> 00:16:17,309
Why?

291
00:16:20,413 --> 00:16:21,413
(exhales)

292
00:16:21,614 --> 00:16:23,048
Do we have a deal or not?

293
00:16:26,252 --> 00:16:27,252
Fine.

294
00:16:30,723 --> 00:16:32,385
- (door opens)
- (sighs)

295
00:16:32,592 --> 00:16:33,592
(gunshot)

296
00:16:37,697 --> 00:16:39,757
I did not make that
building go boom, Jim.

297
00:16:40,466 --> 00:16:41,466
Oh, please!

298
00:16:41,767 --> 00:16:44,236
You gave up any
shred of honor long ago!

299
00:16:44,403 --> 00:16:46,338
Why should we believe
a snake like you?!

300
00:16:46,472 --> 00:16:49,670
Because I would never take
credit for somebody else's work.

301
00:16:50,109 --> 00:16:52,738
Oh, no, you'd just betray
anyone and everyone

302
00:16:53,079 --> 00:16:54,411
for the right price!

303
00:16:55,181 --> 00:16:56,649
Is this about Sofia Falcone?

304
00:16:56,782 --> 00:16:58,293
Because you should
really move past that.

305
00:16:58,317 --> 00:16:59,317
It's not healthy.

306
00:16:59,352 --> 00:17:00,352
(gunshot)

307
00:17:04,056 --> 00:17:05,581
Oswald, concentrate.

308
00:17:06,058 --> 00:17:07,402
Do you and your men
have enough ammo

309
00:17:07,426 --> 00:17:09,395
to keep Zsasz pinned down
for a couple of minutes?

310
00:17:10,563 --> 00:17:13,123
For you, Jim, let's
say... two and a half.

311
00:17:13,432 --> 00:17:15,424
Concentrate all your fire
on that window up there.

312
00:17:15,568 --> 00:17:18,470
Give me everything you
got, then abruptly cease fire.

313
00:17:19,105 --> 00:17:20,232
You can count on me.

314
00:17:20,373 --> 00:17:21,500
Spread the word.

315
00:17:22,608 --> 00:17:24,099
MAN: All right,
guys, gather around,

316
00:17:24,243 --> 00:17:25,506
we got new orders.

317
00:17:26,812 --> 00:17:27,812
All right.

318
00:17:28,414 --> 00:17:29,414
Ready?

319
00:17:29,549 --> 00:17:30,549
Yeah.

320
00:17:33,452 --> 00:17:34,452
Now!

321
00:17:34,520 --> 00:17:36,489
Fire!

322
00:17:58,611 --> 00:18:00,341
Keep it coming!

323
00:18:09,155 --> 00:18:10,817
(slurping)

324
00:18:15,094 --> 00:18:16,426
(watch beeping)

325
00:18:16,562 --> 00:18:17,562
Cease fire!

326
00:18:18,097 --> 00:18:19,097
Cease fire!

327
00:18:19,432 --> 00:18:20,559
Cease fire!

328
00:18:32,878 --> 00:18:34,642
Glad to see
you're still with us.

329
00:18:41,587 --> 00:18:44,216
Thank you. Thank
you. You were great.

330
00:18:44,357 --> 00:18:45,557
Glad there's no hard feelings.

331
00:18:45,591 --> 00:18:47,253
Day's not over yet. Come on.

332
00:18:48,361 --> 00:18:49,385
Well done, Jim.

333
00:18:50,363 --> 00:18:51,626
We make a hell of a team.

334
00:18:52,298 --> 00:18:52,890
That man is a menace.

335
00:18:53,232 --> 00:18:54,325
Allow me to deal with him.

336
00:18:54,634 --> 00:18:56,899
This is about more than just
your vendetta against Zsasz.

337
00:18:57,236 --> 00:18:58,436
If he did this, I need to know

338
00:18:58,571 --> 00:19:00,130
if it was a part of
something larger.

339
00:19:00,740 --> 00:19:02,174
My point exactly.

340
00:19:03,209 --> 00:19:04,541
One of the areas
in which I excel

341
00:19:04,677 --> 00:19:06,669
is the loosening of tongues.

342
00:19:06,879 --> 00:19:08,609
No. He's mine.

343
00:19:16,722 --> 00:19:19,248
(siren wails)

344
00:19:19,392 --> 00:19:22,521
♪♪

345
00:19:45,618 --> 00:19:47,484
(people coughing)

346
00:19:53,225 --> 00:19:55,421
SYKES: You're pushing
my men way too hard.

347
00:19:55,961 --> 00:19:56,758
We're not gonna break through

348
00:19:56,896 --> 00:19:58,728
for at least a couple more days.

349
00:19:59,498 --> 00:20:02,525
There is absolutely no
way to make it on schedule.

350
00:20:04,370 --> 00:20:05,895
(choking)

351
00:20:15,948 --> 00:20:18,884
Well, not with <i>that</i>
attitude, you're not.

352
00:20:21,487 --> 00:20:22,487
Now...

353
00:20:22,788 --> 00:20:23,915
everyone...

354
00:20:24,623 --> 00:20:26,751
let's reach inside and dig...

355
00:20:27,359 --> 00:20:29,794
a little deeper, shall we?

356
00:20:30,763 --> 00:20:31,773
'Cause that's the only way

357
00:20:31,797 --> 00:20:34,494
you're all making
it out of this hole.

358
00:20:45,778 --> 00:20:47,508
Come on. Put this on.

359
00:20:49,682 --> 00:20:53,710
(muttering)

360
00:20:53,853 --> 00:20:55,014
(giggling)

361
00:20:56,689 --> 00:20:57,952
(continues giggling)

362
00:21:00,860 --> 00:21:01,919
(clears throat)

363
00:21:02,962 --> 00:21:04,954
Are these all the recruits?

364
00:21:06,799 --> 00:21:08,734
Well, I thought you
would want quality

365
00:21:08,868 --> 00:21:09,868
over quantity.

366
00:21:10,336 --> 00:21:13,363
Not everybody can pass
a .38 caliber test of faith.

367
00:21:14,740 --> 00:21:15,901
(gasps)

368
00:21:18,978 --> 00:21:20,276
Yes...

369
00:21:20,813 --> 00:21:24,341
you certainly have
set a very high bar

370
00:21:24,483 --> 00:21:26,008
- for devotion.
- (gasps)

371
00:21:26,652 --> 00:21:27,813
(sighs)

372
00:21:28,554 --> 00:21:30,455
Oh. Almost forgot.

373
00:21:30,823 --> 00:21:33,383
Bruce Wayne and
his sidekick Curls...

374
00:21:34,360 --> 00:21:36,295
Or is <i>he</i> the sidekick? (giggles)

375
00:21:36,428 --> 00:21:39,887
Anyway, they tried to infiltrate
our little operation here.

376
00:21:40,332 --> 00:21:41,595
- Oh?
- Oh.

377
00:21:41,967 --> 00:21:44,596
And Curls can walk, really well,

378
00:21:44,770 --> 00:21:45,770
especially...

379
00:21:46,438 --> 00:21:47,633
for a paraplegic.

380
00:21:50,409 --> 00:21:52,742
Ah. And she wants to kill you.

381
00:21:53,479 --> 00:21:56,074
A lot, FYI.

382
00:21:57,483 --> 00:21:59,577
If I see her, I'll
give you a shout.

383
00:22:00,019 --> 00:22:02,955
Oh... and kill her.

384
00:22:03,355 --> 00:22:04,355
Mm.

385
00:22:12,498 --> 00:22:14,023
Okay, recruits,

386
00:22:14,366 --> 00:22:15,664
let's do like my daddy did

387
00:22:15,801 --> 00:22:18,771
before my sixth
birthday and move out.

388
00:22:46,432 --> 00:22:47,432
Ed?

389
00:22:50,002 --> 00:22:53,131
The building's
superstructure is largely intact,

390
00:22:53,772 --> 00:22:56,401
which would rule
out C-4 and Semtex.

391
00:22:58,444 --> 00:23:00,504
Evidence of deflagration
would suggest something

392
00:23:00,646 --> 00:23:03,673
with a slower burn
rate, like gunpowder

393
00:23:03,816 --> 00:23:05,375
or nitroglycerin.

394
00:23:05,517 --> 00:23:06,828
But for this level
of destruction,

395
00:23:06,852 --> 00:23:08,129
that would require
a bomb that's...

396
00:23:08,153 --> 00:23:09,951
20 cubic feet of
explosive material.

397
00:23:10,089 --> 00:23:12,133
There's no way you could sneak
something that size into the building.

398
00:23:12,157 --> 00:23:15,093
All the entrances were
guarded, which suggests maybe

399
00:23:15,427 --> 00:23:16,918
that this was an inside job.

400
00:23:18,564 --> 00:23:19,564
Or...

401
00:23:21,533 --> 00:23:23,695
a classic locked-room mystery.

402
00:23:24,703 --> 00:23:27,969
Man walks into a room, alone,
and is later found murdered.

403
00:23:28,140 --> 00:23:30,632
There are no
windows, and one door,

404
00:23:30,809 --> 00:23:33,074
which is locked from the inside.

405
00:23:33,846 --> 00:23:35,815
The killer was already
inside the room.

406
00:23:36,048 --> 00:23:38,142
So, the bomb was
already inside the building?

407
00:23:38,717 --> 00:23:39,717
No.

408
00:23:39,885 --> 00:23:42,912
The bomb <i>was</i> the building.

409
00:23:43,055 --> 00:23:44,614
You don't need to
smuggle in explosives

410
00:23:44,757 --> 00:23:46,851
when you can
detonate 250 gallons

411
00:23:46,992 --> 00:23:48,961
of highly pressurized
heating oil

412
00:23:49,094 --> 00:23:50,790
that's coursing
through all the floors.

413
00:23:51,130 --> 00:23:53,998
True, but that
doesn't explain this.

414
00:23:55,567 --> 00:23:56,091
Glass?

415
00:23:56,435 --> 00:23:57,767
Presumably from there.

416
00:24:00,839 --> 00:24:01,966
No, there's no way that that

417
00:24:02,107 --> 00:24:03,871
would blast in
with that explosion.

418
00:24:04,009 --> 00:24:06,249
That would make all of that
glass go out onto the street.

419
00:24:06,445 --> 00:24:08,641
Unless whatever ignited
those heating oil tanks...

420
00:24:08,781 --> 00:24:10,113
BOTH: Smashed
through that window.

421
00:24:14,019 --> 00:24:15,019
Ow.

422
00:24:15,955 --> 00:24:17,446
This is a really nice table.

423
00:24:17,856 --> 00:24:19,688
We got a dozen
witnesses that saw you

424
00:24:19,825 --> 00:24:22,090
walk out of that building
before it went kabooey.

425
00:24:22,628 --> 00:24:24,961
Yeah. I heard some
gangs had taken over.

426
00:24:25,497 --> 00:24:27,090
Figured, with you guys occupied,

427
00:24:27,232 --> 00:24:29,497
I might help myself to
some of your supplies.

428
00:24:29,768 --> 00:24:31,368
Hey, do you guys have
any canned peaches?

429
00:24:31,637 --> 00:24:34,106
Man, I'd trade an arm
and a leg for that right now.

430
00:24:34,807 --> 00:24:36,867
Not mine, somebody else's.

431
00:24:38,677 --> 00:24:40,543
If you're innocent, why shoot up

432
00:24:40,679 --> 00:24:42,011
a city block full of cops?

433
00:24:42,915 --> 00:24:46,010
Because it was full of cops,

434
00:24:46,485 --> 00:24:48,181
who were also
trying to shoot me.

435
00:24:48,520 --> 00:24:50,250
And, guys, those were
warning shots. I mean,

436
00:24:50,589 --> 00:24:52,581
if I really wanted
to kill you...

437
00:24:53,959 --> 00:24:56,258
- you'd be dead.
- You got a pen?

438
00:24:56,795 --> 00:24:58,661
I want to write this
guy a thank-you letter.

439
00:24:58,931 --> 00:25:00,661
ZSASZ: Do the math.

440
00:25:00,833 --> 00:25:02,699
If I blew up a
building full of people,

441
00:25:03,002 --> 00:25:05,164
I would have covered
every inch of my body

442
00:25:05,504 --> 00:25:07,063
in sweet, sweet scars.

443
00:25:07,673 --> 00:25:09,039
You guys want to
do a strip search?

444
00:25:09,508 --> 00:25:10,703
(door opens)

445
00:25:11,176 --> 00:25:12,872
Got Lucius on the
horn for you, Cap.

446
00:25:13,746 --> 00:25:14,770
Stay on him.

447
00:25:17,683 --> 00:25:19,208
I'd let Alvarez do it.

448
00:25:20,285 --> 00:25:21,583
He's handsome.

449
00:25:22,721 --> 00:25:23,984
Lucius, talk to me.

450
00:25:24,256 --> 00:25:25,952
LUCIUS: Haven wasn't
destroyed by a bomb.

451
00:25:26,091 --> 00:25:28,560
It was an RPG, like the one
that took down the chopper.

452
00:25:29,161 --> 00:25:29,958
You sure?

453
00:25:30,129 --> 00:25:32,107
I'm holding what's left
of it in my hand right now.

454
00:25:32,131 --> 00:25:33,656
We found pieces
of it in the rubble.

455
00:25:34,133 --> 00:25:35,733
It was fired through
the basement window,

456
00:25:35,768 --> 00:25:37,236
detonated the fuel oil tank.

457
00:25:37,569 --> 00:25:38,947
And we're still trying
to figure out exactly

458
00:25:38,971 --> 00:25:40,633
which rooftop it was fired from.

459
00:25:40,773 --> 00:25:42,264
- Rooftop?
- LUCIUS: Yes.

460
00:25:42,608 --> 00:25:45,009
The only angle you could
hit this tank from is above.

461
00:25:45,611 --> 00:25:47,204
Our only suspect
was on the ground.

462
00:25:47,546 --> 00:25:48,757
Zsasz was seen
leaving the building

463
00:25:48,781 --> 00:25:49,942
moments before it blew.

464
00:25:51,016 --> 00:25:52,951
Looks like you
need a new suspect.

465
00:25:54,286 --> 00:25:56,187
Jim! (chuckles)

466
00:25:57,222 --> 00:25:59,623
Ah. I know the wheels of justice

467
00:25:59,758 --> 00:26:01,818
turn slowly, so
I'm here to provide

468
00:26:01,960 --> 00:26:03,861
- a modicum of grease.
- Oswald,

469
00:26:04,129 --> 00:26:05,791
you need to leave right now.

470
00:26:05,931 --> 00:26:08,059
Still claiming he's
innocent, is he?

471
00:26:08,200 --> 00:26:10,726
Yes. And as much
as I hate to admit it,

472
00:26:10,869 --> 00:26:12,201
the evidence is backing him up.

473
00:26:12,337 --> 00:26:13,337
What the hell's going on?

474
00:26:13,639 --> 00:26:14,834
Harvey, according to Lucius,

475
00:26:14,940 --> 00:26:16,966
- Zsasz couldn't have done it.
- (Oswald sighs)

476
00:26:17,342 --> 00:26:20,904
I did not expect
you to go soft, Jim.

477
00:26:22,614 --> 00:26:23,274
Actually, I did.

478
00:26:23,615 --> 00:26:25,335
Which is why I didn't
come alone. (whistles)

479
00:26:25,651 --> 00:26:28,177
- ♪♪
- (chuckles)

480
00:26:36,261 --> 00:26:37,786
Bring me Victor Zsasz.

481
00:26:39,331 --> 00:26:40,663
Torturing...

482
00:26:43,001 --> 00:26:46,961
Torturing Zsasz into confessing
won't give the people justice.

483
00:26:47,639 --> 00:26:49,631
As misguided as he is,

484
00:26:49,942 --> 00:26:52,639
my old friend Jim is
correct in this instance.

485
00:26:53,112 --> 00:26:55,672
Despite our enflamed passions,

486
00:26:55,814 --> 00:26:58,113
we cannot rush to judgment.

487
00:26:58,851 --> 00:27:01,343
- There will be a trial.
- MEN: Yeah!

488
00:27:01,920 --> 00:27:04,788
And we will let
the people decide.

489
00:27:04,890 --> 00:27:06,290
MEN: Yeah!

490
00:27:07,159 --> 00:27:10,095
Good to know who's
really in charge here, Jim.

491
00:27:29,381 --> 00:27:31,145
LUCIUS: This is
it, the exact place

492
00:27:31,283 --> 00:27:33,115
where the RPG was fired.

493
00:27:35,087 --> 00:27:36,087
Impressive.

494
00:27:36,421 --> 00:27:37,719
Calculated the angle of incident

495
00:27:37,856 --> 00:27:40,883
to follow the trajectory through
the window, into the fuel oil tanks.

496
00:27:41,326 --> 00:27:42,326
Yep.

497
00:27:43,061 --> 00:27:45,895
That, and the RPG
case is right over there.

498
00:27:55,407 --> 00:27:57,273
So, now we know how it was done;

499
00:27:58,210 --> 00:28:00,441
hopefully this holds
some clues as to who.

500
00:28:05,284 --> 00:28:06,752
I should get this
back to the lab.

501
00:28:07,019 --> 00:28:08,196
Doubtful that someone
who could pull off

502
00:28:08,220 --> 00:28:11,213
such an intricate plot
would forget to wear gloves.

503
00:28:11,890 --> 00:28:13,859
True, but it's all
I've got to go on.

504
00:28:14,126 --> 00:28:16,960
Well, maybe I'm wrong.

505
00:28:18,430 --> 00:28:19,796
I hope so.

506
00:28:21,967 --> 00:28:24,698
I truly hope that you
find whoever did this...

507
00:28:25,804 --> 00:28:27,295
and you make them pay.

508
00:28:32,377 --> 00:28:33,868
As promised.

509
00:28:36,248 --> 00:28:37,807
I appreciate your help, Ed.

510
00:28:38,116 --> 00:28:39,436
Couldn't have
done it without you.

511
00:28:40,219 --> 00:28:42,916
And if you tell anyone
I said that, I will deny it.

512
00:28:48,360 --> 00:28:52,263
"Inmate number
1215. Frank McCann."

513
00:28:53,031 --> 00:28:54,294
(quietly): Frank McCann.

514
00:28:55,000 --> 00:28:57,026
Deceased? No!

515
00:28:57,769 --> 00:29:01,171
No! No, you can't be dead!

516
00:29:01,740 --> 00:29:04,938
(shouts) No!

517
00:29:05,244 --> 00:29:06,268
(shouts)

518
00:29:13,085 --> 00:29:15,316
Lucius! Hey!

519
00:29:15,754 --> 00:29:19,054
Di... Did you see who
blew up this building?

520
00:29:19,992 --> 00:29:21,961
Hey! (groans)

521
00:29:22,961 --> 00:29:24,327
Deaf old bat.

522
00:29:25,831 --> 00:29:27,527
Hopefully she's not blind, too.

523
00:29:32,137 --> 00:29:33,799
(excited chatter)

524
00:29:33,939 --> 00:29:35,032
(door opens)

525
00:29:37,943 --> 00:29:39,002
MAN: There he is.

526
00:29:39,144 --> 00:29:41,306
(chatter continues)

527
00:29:44,249 --> 00:29:45,529
BULLOCK: I got
no love for Zsasz,

528
00:29:45,851 --> 00:29:47,547
but are we really
gonna let this happen?

529
00:29:48,220 --> 00:29:49,518
Look at them, Harvey.

530
00:29:50,522 --> 00:29:54,482
If we try to stop it, they'll
turn that anger against us.

531
00:29:55,160 --> 00:29:56,253
We've got to do something.

532
00:29:56,395 --> 00:29:58,296
Yeah? Like what?

533
00:29:59,231 --> 00:30:01,063
I make another speech?

534
00:30:03,168 --> 00:30:05,330
Maybe this is what
the people need.

535
00:30:08,140 --> 00:30:10,166
- (gavel bangs)
- Order in the court!

536
00:30:10,942 --> 00:30:14,379
So, um, will I be
appointed a lawyer?

537
00:30:14,546 --> 00:30:17,015
I feel like my rights
are being violated.

538
00:30:17,249 --> 00:30:20,048
Not your right to
remain silent. Bailiff.

539
00:30:20,185 --> 00:30:22,484
No, no, no, no, no...

540
00:30:26,358 --> 00:30:30,227
And where exactly did
you see the defendant

541
00:30:30,562 --> 00:30:32,463
minutes before the explosion?

542
00:30:33,131 --> 00:30:34,565
Coming out of the building.

543
00:30:35,434 --> 00:30:38,529
The very building that
mere moments later

544
00:30:38,870 --> 00:30:41,430
was engulfed in
flames that would claim

545
00:30:41,840 --> 00:30:43,968
so many beloved souls?

546
00:30:44,343 --> 00:30:46,141
(crying)

547
00:30:46,311 --> 00:30:48,143
(quiet chatter)

548
00:30:54,886 --> 00:30:55,546
Are there any other witnesses

549
00:30:55,887 --> 00:30:57,131
who can back up
this man's claim? Oh.

550
00:30:57,155 --> 00:30:58,214
- Here.
- Yep. Here.

551
00:31:01,360 --> 00:31:03,192
(chuckles weakly)

552
00:31:04,596 --> 00:31:07,589
Captain Gordon would
have this court believe

553
00:31:07,933 --> 00:31:11,370
that all of you fine
citizens are mistaken.

554
00:31:11,503 --> 00:31:13,369
(crowd exclaiming)

555
00:31:13,572 --> 00:31:17,873
He claims that Mr. Zsasz is
not responsible for the bombing.

556
00:31:18,043 --> 00:31:20,137
(crowd exclaiming)

557
00:31:24,983 --> 00:31:27,350
Captain Gordon, if you
would like to say something,

558
00:31:27,552 --> 00:31:28,952
now is the time.

559
00:31:29,087 --> 00:31:31,147
(overlapping chatter)

560
00:31:32,190 --> 00:31:33,590
MAN: Tell us something.

561
00:31:34,025 --> 00:31:35,926
♪♪

562
00:31:38,397 --> 00:31:39,922
It wasn't a bomb.

563
00:31:39,998 --> 00:31:42,467
(crowd exclaiming)

564
00:31:43,468 --> 00:31:45,027
GORDON: It wasn't a bomb.

565
00:31:45,170 --> 00:31:47,105
Shh, shh, shh, shh, shh, shh.

566
00:31:47,272 --> 00:31:51,073
A rocket-propelled
grenade, fired from a rooftop.

567
00:31:52,511 --> 00:31:56,209
If Zsasz was on the ground,
as your witnesses claim,

568
00:31:57,182 --> 00:31:58,616
he couldn't have done it.

569
00:32:05,390 --> 00:32:07,291
I know you all want justice.

570
00:32:08,193 --> 00:32:09,354
So do I.

571
00:32:10,562 --> 00:32:11,586
You're angry.

572
00:32:13,632 --> 00:32:15,066
You're scared.

573
00:32:16,401 --> 00:32:18,336
For months now, you've
been hearing me say

574
00:32:18,570 --> 00:32:20,129
help is coming.

575
00:32:22,441 --> 00:32:24,535
Truth is, we may be on our own.

576
00:32:27,612 --> 00:32:29,308
And if that's true,

577
00:32:30,582 --> 00:32:33,984
what we do now is
more important than ever.

578
00:32:35,954 --> 00:32:37,013
This...

579
00:32:38,957 --> 00:32:40,585
This is not justice.

580
00:32:43,161 --> 00:32:45,027
This is not justice.

581
00:32:47,999 --> 00:32:49,558
It's not who we are.

582
00:32:53,605 --> 00:32:54,368
I'll consider that

583
00:32:54,506 --> 00:32:56,304
your closing argument.

584
00:32:57,642 --> 00:33:03,013
Now that the defense has
rested, let's put it to the crowd.

585
00:33:05,317 --> 00:33:08,185
What say you, jury?

586
00:33:13,291 --> 00:33:16,284
CROWD: Guilty!

587
00:33:16,461 --> 00:33:18,589
(overlapping shouting)

588
00:33:18,997 --> 00:33:21,660
(chanting): Guilty!
Guilty! Guilty!

589
00:33:25,370 --> 00:33:27,339
(chanting continues)

590
00:33:34,713 --> 00:33:38,309
(cheering, shouting)

591
00:33:49,194 --> 00:33:50,321
(gavel banging)

592
00:33:53,098 --> 00:33:54,657
By the power vested in me

593
00:33:55,267 --> 00:33:57,759
by... well, me,

594
00:33:58,570 --> 00:34:02,200
I sentence you, Victor Zsasz,

595
00:34:02,707 --> 00:34:04,039
to die.

596
00:34:05,577 --> 00:34:07,045
Any last words?

597
00:34:08,713 --> 00:34:14,744
(muffled mumbling)

598
00:34:15,654 --> 00:34:16,713
Well said.

599
00:34:17,055 --> 00:34:19,354
- (people screaming)
- Get him out of there.

600
00:34:22,627 --> 00:34:23,788
Overruled!

601
00:34:27,399 --> 00:34:29,561
What the hell are you doing?!

602
00:34:29,701 --> 00:34:32,136
Trying to keep you all from
making a terrible mistake.

603
00:34:32,270 --> 00:34:33,270
OSWALD: See?

604
00:34:33,471 --> 00:34:39,240
Jim Gordon cares more
about protecting a murderer

605
00:34:39,511 --> 00:34:41,446
than he does about
protecting you!

606
00:34:42,280 --> 00:34:43,543
You've lost them, Jim.

607
00:34:44,583 --> 00:34:46,484
They don't believe
in you anymore.

608
00:34:47,586 --> 00:34:49,145
They're mine now.

609
00:35:05,337 --> 00:35:06,168
BULLOCK: I got
to be honest, Jim,

610
00:35:06,304 --> 00:35:09,138
I don't know how safe
Zsasz will be at the GCPD.

611
00:35:09,808 --> 00:35:12,607
There's more than a few cops
that want to see him on a slab.

612
00:35:13,311 --> 00:35:15,111
GORDON: That's why
we're not taking him there.

613
00:35:16,581 --> 00:35:19,813
Wait. Tell me this is
not a catch and release.

614
00:35:20,151 --> 00:35:22,848
What choice do I
have? Either I let him go,

615
00:35:23,421 --> 00:35:25,356
or he's dead for
something he didn't do.

616
00:35:33,698 --> 00:35:34,698
Thank you, Jim.

617
00:35:35,166 --> 00:35:36,259
I'm not doing this for you.

618
00:35:36,534 --> 00:35:37,593
Of course not.

619
00:35:37,902 --> 00:35:40,770
This city will never be
what you want it to be, Jim.

620
00:35:41,806 --> 00:35:46,244
It's always gonna belong
to the bad guys, like me.

621
00:35:53,852 --> 00:35:55,650
Give him your gun, Harvey.

622
00:35:56,521 --> 00:35:57,284
What?

623
00:35:57,422 --> 00:35:58,720
Yeah. What?

624
00:36:00,392 --> 00:36:01,792
Give him your gun.

625
00:36:03,461 --> 00:36:04,190
Jim.

626
00:36:04,329 --> 00:36:06,764
- Do it!
- ZSASZ: You got a
death wish, Jim?

627
00:36:07,932 --> 00:36:08,932
'Cause I'll kill you.

628
00:36:09,901 --> 00:36:10,901
Harvey, too.

629
00:36:11,703 --> 00:36:12,703
Maybe.

630
00:36:13,738 --> 00:36:16,298
Maybe I'm just tired of
listening to you, Victor.

631
00:36:22,681 --> 00:36:23,740
Do it.

632
00:36:30,955 --> 00:36:32,617
You know what? You seem tired.

633
00:36:34,459 --> 00:36:35,459
Maybe we should do this

634
00:36:35,560 --> 00:36:37,324
another day, yeah?

635
00:36:39,798 --> 00:36:42,563
People like you are
always trying to own this city.

636
00:36:43,201 --> 00:36:44,567
But you never will.

637
00:36:45,670 --> 00:36:47,901
Now get the hell out of my face.

638
00:36:50,775 --> 00:36:52,300
See you around, Jim.

639
00:37:00,485 --> 00:37:01,612
A shoot-out?

640
00:37:01,920 --> 00:37:03,764
- Are you serious?
- What? I could've taken him.

641
00:37:03,788 --> 00:37:05,780
That's not the point. Never...

642
00:37:06,691 --> 00:37:09,388
ever ask me to do
anything like that again.

643
00:37:10,495 --> 00:37:12,225
Pull yourself together.

644
00:37:28,880 --> 00:37:30,906
(grunting, screaming)

645
00:37:48,533 --> 00:37:54,268
You see, a river cuts through
rock not because of its power,

646
00:37:54,405 --> 00:37:56,931
but because of its persistence.

647
00:37:57,609 --> 00:38:00,443
So what do we do when
we feel like giving up?

648
00:38:00,578 --> 00:38:02,046
WORKERS: Dig a little deeper.

649
00:38:02,380 --> 00:38:06,875
And what do we do when we
can't possibly go on any longer?

650
00:38:07,018 --> 00:38:08,953
WORKERS: Dig a little deeper.

651
00:38:09,287 --> 00:38:10,550
And what do we...

652
00:38:13,825 --> 00:38:14,690
SELINA: Deep enough?

653
00:38:14,826 --> 00:38:16,727
(gasping)

654
00:38:21,666 --> 00:38:25,034
(grunting)

655
00:38:25,904 --> 00:38:27,372
(straining): Well, Selina,

656
00:38:27,739 --> 00:38:28,900
I must say...

657
00:38:29,374 --> 00:38:30,374
(gasps)

658
00:38:30,742 --> 00:38:34,838
Don't say anything.

659
00:38:41,719 --> 00:38:42,812
- (shouts)
- BRUCE: Selina!

660
00:38:42,954 --> 00:38:44,547
- Selina!
- Let me go!

661
00:38:44,689 --> 00:38:46,590
BRUCE: It's done! It's over.

662
00:38:46,858 --> 00:38:48,383
MAN (distant): Jeremiah!

663
00:38:48,927 --> 00:38:50,623
We need to get out of here, now.

664
00:39:03,641 --> 00:39:06,668
(people shouting, coughing)

665
00:39:20,091 --> 00:39:21,423
(Nygma panting)

666
00:39:27,365 --> 00:39:28,594
I hate stairs.

667
00:39:34,439 --> 00:39:35,907
Oh, God.

668
00:39:36,875 --> 00:39:39,606
(wheels squeaking)

669
00:39:41,679 --> 00:39:44,649
(gasps) Oh, no! Go away!

670
00:39:44,782 --> 00:39:47,809
No... hey, wait. I need
to know what you know.

671
00:39:48,486 --> 00:39:51,513
Oh, please. Oh, oh,
please don't hurt me.

672
00:39:51,656 --> 00:39:54,558
Listen, lady, I'm
not gonna hurt you.

673
00:39:55,760 --> 00:39:58,730
I just want to
know what you saw.

674
00:39:59,697 --> 00:40:01,723
Uh, uh, the-the roof.

675
00:40:01,900 --> 00:40:03,664
You, you were on the roof.

676
00:40:03,801 --> 00:40:06,430
And you had some kind of a, uh,

677
00:40:06,571 --> 00:40:09,541
you had some kind
of a, of a rocket.

678
00:40:10,942 --> 00:40:11,705
Mm.

679
00:40:11,910 --> 00:40:13,173
That's, that's not possible.

680
00:40:13,511 --> 00:40:17,915
Uh, and-and you shot
it at-at-at that building.

681
00:40:20,852 --> 00:40:21,852
Oh!

682
00:40:26,491 --> 00:40:28,187
♪♪

683
00:40:31,162 --> 00:40:33,063
(rapid beeping)

684
00:40:33,564 --> 00:40:34,964
(panting)

685
00:40:36,634 --> 00:40:37,634
Oh!

686
00:41:01,025 --> 00:41:03,688
Oh, I'm sorry.

687
00:41:04,195 --> 00:41:05,219
I promise,

688
00:41:06,164 --> 00:41:08,463
I won't tell anybody.

689
00:41:09,834 --> 00:41:11,462
I know you won't.

690
00:41:14,105 --> 00:41:16,734
You said you wouldn't hurt me.

691
00:41:17,842 --> 00:41:18,842
I'm really very sorry.

692
00:41:18,977 --> 00:41:22,038
Oh, no! Oh, no! (screaming)

693
00:41:26,050 --> 00:41:28,042
(car alarm blaring)

694
00:41:34,125 --> 00:41:35,821
(siren wailing in distance)

695
00:41:46,537 --> 00:41:47,937
BARBARA: Spotlight's off.

696
00:41:49,207 --> 00:41:51,005
You forget to pay
the electric bill?

697
00:41:52,010 --> 00:41:53,501
What do you want, Barbara?

698
00:41:54,045 --> 00:41:55,172
Same as you.

699
00:41:56,147 --> 00:41:57,911
To protect my people.

700
00:41:58,649 --> 00:42:00,709
Your tip didn't pan out.

701
00:42:02,520 --> 00:42:03,954
Well, I've got another one.

702
00:42:05,923 --> 00:42:08,085
A lead on a guy selling RPGs.

703
00:42:08,926 --> 00:42:10,189
He moves about, but...

704
00:42:12,296 --> 00:42:13,525
That all?

705
00:42:16,034 --> 00:42:18,799
I heard some of your
people went over to Penguin.

706
00:42:20,538 --> 00:42:21,538
Yeah.

707
00:42:22,807 --> 00:42:24,298
Well, you can't win them all.

708
00:42:26,177 --> 00:42:27,736
Poor Jim.

709
00:42:28,713 --> 00:42:30,306
All alone again.

710
00:42:33,251 --> 00:42:35,049
Get out of here, Barbara.

711
00:42:39,690 --> 00:42:41,750
No one knows what
it's like to be him.

712
00:42:45,063 --> 00:42:47,089
To carry the weight.

713
00:42:49,200 --> 00:42:50,862
I told you to leave.

714
00:42:52,870 --> 00:42:54,304
I heard you.
